{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 6.2 PDF export options\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Client-side PDF generation: use a deterministic HTML-to-PDF library (e.g., print CSS + browser 'Save as PDF') or a JS library. Pros: fast to ship; cons: inconsistent across browsers.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Server-side PDF rendering: Node service (Playwright/Chromium) renders the proposal route and outputs a PDF stored in S3. Pros: consistent output; cons: heavier infra.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Recommendation for v1: Server-side Playwright PDF export behind an API endpoint for consistency; cache PDFs per proposal version in S3.\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 7. React Framework Recommendation\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\fs22 Use Next.js (React framework) for the customer portal. It provides routing, SSR/SSG options, good DX, and easy deployment to serverless or containers. We can still host the frontend as static assets and call a separate API; Next can also host API routes, but we recommend keeping a dedicated Node API for clarity and future auth/payment integrations.\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Next.js App Router for modern routing/layout; strict TypeScript; ESLint + Prettier.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Component library: lightweight (e.g., Radix UI + Tailwind) for speed; ensure accessibility.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Responsive-first design: mobile viewport, touch targets, performance budgets.\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 8. Backend (Node.js/TypeScript) Recommendation\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab API runtime: Node.js/TypeScript with Express (fastest given existing stack) or Fastify for better typing/perf. Keep v1 aligned with existing Express expertise.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Validation: zod or joi for request validation; typed DTOs.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab DB access: Prisma or knex. Prisma recommended for velocity and migrations.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Security middleware: helmet, cors configuration, rate limiting (e.g., express-rate-limit), structured logging (pino).\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 9. Integration Points with Existing Sales System\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 9.1 Recommended integration pattern (v1)\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\fs22 Avoid exposing the existing admin portal directly to customers. Instead, add a controlled integration layer where the sales system publishes proposal metadata + assets into the customer portal data stores.\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Option A (recommended): Sales system emits events on proposal creation/update (e.g., via queue/webhook) to Customer Portal API which upserts metadata + writes assets to S3.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Option B: Scheduled sync job (polling) that queries sales DB/API and imports new/updated proposals; simplest if events are hard to add quickly.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Option C: Shared database access (least preferred) where portal reads from existing DB tables; higher coupling and risk.\par}

{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 9.2 Concrete integration touchpoints\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Sales Admin Portal → Customer Portal: on proposal 'Publish to customer', generate structured payload + optional PDF; upload to S3; write metadata row in portal DB.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Identifier mapping: sales lead record must map to portal customer_id (email/phone). Define canonical identifier early.\par}
