{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 10. Infrastructure, Deployment & IaC\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 10.1 Deployment model\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Frontend: Next.js static export or standard Next build deployed to CDN (e.g., S3+CloudFront, Vercel, Netlify). Prefer S3+CloudFront if keeping everything in one cloud account.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab API: Node.js/TypeScript deployed via containers (ECS/Fargate or Kubernetes) OR serverless functions. For Playwright PDF export, containers are usually easier.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Database: managed PostgreSQL (RDS or equivalent).\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Object storage: S3 with bucket policies; server-side encryption enabled.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Secrets: managed secrets store (AWS Secrets Manager) and KMS for encryption keys.\par}

{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 10.2 Infrastructure-as-Code\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Use Terraform (or Pulumi) to define: VPC/networking (if needed), RDS, S3 buckets, CloudFront, ECS services, IAM roles/policies, alarms, logs.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Separate environments: dev/staging/prod; minimal but real staging environment recommended within 6 weeks.\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 11. CI/CD (GitHub Actions)\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Pipelines: (1) PR checks, (2) deploy staging on merge to main, (3) promote to prod with manual approval.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Checks: TypeScript build, lint, unit tests, API contract tests, frontend build, migration checks.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Security: dependency scanning (Dependabot), secret scanning, minimal SAST (CodeQL).\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Deploy: build container images, push to registry, update service; run DB migrations as a controlled step.\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 12. Repo Strategy: New Repo vs Existing Monorepo\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\sb120\b\fs24 Recommendation\b0\par}

{\pard\sa120\fs22 Create a new repo for the Customer Portal (frontend + API + IaC) unless the existing org already has a stable monorepo toolchain. A new repo reduces coupling and keeps the portal’s security boundary and deployment lifecycle independent.\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab New repo pros: clearer ownership, simpler CI/CD, safer separation from internal admin code, easier to open-source common UI later if desired.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Monorepo pros: shared types/components, atomic changes across sales system + portal, single dependency graph.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 \bullet\tab Compromise: keep separate repos but publish shared types as an internal npm package later.\par}

{\pard\sa160\sb160\b\fs28 13. Delivery Plan (6 weeks) — Suggested Milestones\b0\par}

{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 1. Week 1: Finalize requirements + identifier strategy; decide integration option (A/B); IaC scaffolding; repo setup; CI baseline.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 2. Week 2: Auth (bootstrap + TOTP), session management, basic portal shell + responsive layout; proposal list/detail screens with mocked data.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 3. Week 3: Integration from sales system to portal DB/S3; strict authorization; audit logging.\par}
{\pard\li360\sa80\fs22 4. Week 4: Proposal rendering from structured payload; server-side PDF export via Playwright; caching to S3.\par}
